    <h1><em>The Celts</em> documentary</h1>


<a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0000WN10E?tag=enylyrallenys-20&camp=0&creative=0&linkCode=as1&creativeASIN=B0000WN10E&adid=0SZED06FETQ0ZM78A5FX&" class="new-amazon-album-link" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank"><Image src={tcdvd} alt=""  width="200" height="200" />
<p><em>The Celts DVD box.</em></p>
</a>


<p>Originally broadcast on <strong>BBC2</strong> in May and June 1987, <strong><em>The Celts: Rich Traditions and Ancient Myths</em></strong> is a landmark six-part documentary series produced by the <strong>BBC</strong>.</p>

<p>Directed by <strong>David Richardson</strong>, the series was written and presented by Irish author <strong>Frank Delaney</strong>, who also published a six-chapter companion book enriched with legendary tales from <strong>Irish mythology</strong>.</p>

<h2>Overview and Production Details</h2>

<p>The documentary examines the historical origins, growth, and enduring cultural impact of Celtic civilization across Great Britain and continental Europe.</p>

<p>A 39-minute selection of the soundtrack was originally released in 1987 as the debut album <a href="/enya-album/"><em>Enya</em></a>, which was later remastered and reissued as <a href="/the-celts/"><em>The Celts</em></a> in 1992.</p>

<p>The entire six-part series was officially <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0000WN10E?tag=enylyrallenys-20&camp=0&creative=0&linkCode=as1&creativeASIN=B0000WN10E&adid=0SZED06FETQ0ZM78A5FX&">released on DVD</a> in Europe and North America in 2004, featuring archived bonus material.</p>

<h2>The Six Episodes</h2>

<p>Broadcast in hour-long installments, each episode explores specific archaeological periods, historical struggles, and artistic traditions of the Celts.</p>


<ol>
<li>
<strong><em>The Man with the Golden Shoes</em></strong> — Examines the earliest archaeological and historical evidence for Celtic civilization, focusing on the two core cultural eras: the <strong>Hallstatt culture</strong> and <strong>La Tène culture</strong>.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", "March of the Celts", "Epona", "Severed Head", "Boadicea", "Spaghetti Western", "Bard Dance", "Sword Fight", and "To Go Beyond I".</em>
</li>

<li>
<strong><em>The Birth of Nations</em></strong> — Traces the emergence and formation of the modern Celtic nations from the territorial changes following the fall of the <strong>Roman Empire</strong>.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", untitled cue ("Over Scarecrow"), "Deserted House", "Deireadh An Tuath", "Boadicea", and "The Sun In The Stream".</em>
</li>

<li>
<strong><em>A Pagan Trinity</em></strong> — Explores ancient <strong>Celtic mythology</strong>, spiritual folklore, druidic legends, and the subsequent spread of early <strong>Christianity</strong> among the Irish and Scots.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", "Fairy Tale I", "Morrighan", "Severed Head", untitled cue, "Triad (Cú Chulainn, Oisín, St. Patrick)", "Deireadh An Tuath", "Fairy Tale II", and "Fairy Tale III".</em>
</li>

<li>
<strong><em>The Open-Ended Curve</em></strong> — Highlights the distinctive physical art, intricate metalwork, and aesthetic achievements of the Celts from ancient times to the present day.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", untitled cue, "March of the Celts", "Triad (St. Patrick)", "Portrait", "I Want Tomorrow", and "To Go Beyond I".</em>
</li>

<li>
<strong><em>The Final Conflict</em></strong> — Details historical conflicts and conquest by neighboring England and France, covering the suppression of the <strong>Welsh language</strong>, Irish revolution, and transatlantic migration to <strong>North America</strong>.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", "Dan y Dŵr", "To Go Beyond II", "The Sun In The Stream", "Deireadh An Tuath", and untitled cue ("Over Funerary Cart").</em>
</li>

<li>
<strong><em>The Legacy</em></strong> — Discusses modern Celtic self-identity, highlighting the <strong>Welsh Eisteddfod</strong>, language survival efforts in <strong>Brittany</strong> and <strong>Cape Breton</strong>, contemporary art, and scenes filmed in <strong>Portmeirion</strong> referencing <em>The Prisoner</em>.<br />
<em>Songs used: "The Celts", "Seaside Ditty", "Third Man", untitled cue, "Irish Piece", "To Go Beyond II", "Deserted House", "Aldebaran", and "Portrait".</em>
</li>
</ol>

<h2><em>The Celts</em> and Enya</h2>


<a href="/video-i-want-tomorrow/" class="new-amazon-album-link" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank"><Image src={img250pxThecelts} alt=""  width="250" height="250" />
<p><em>Enya performs "I Want Tomorrow" during a musical interlude in The Celts.</em></p>
</a>

<p>The documentary is widely recognized for introducing Irish musician <strong>Enya</strong> to a global mainstream audience following her departure from the folk group <a href="/enya-clannad/">Clannad</a>.</p>

<p>In 1985, BBC producer <strong>Tony McAuley</strong> initially asked Enya to write a single track for the series, leading her to submit "March of the Celts".</p>

<p>Director <strong>David Richardson</strong> liked the track so much that he abandoned the idea of hiring multiple composers and commissioned Enya to score all six episodes.</p>

<h3>Score Composition and Visual Performance</h3>

<p>Working alongside producer <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> and the BBC studios in <strong>Wood Lane, London</strong>, Enya recorded 72 minutes of music for the project.</p>

<p>Granted complete creative control, she established her trademark musical sound characterized by complex vocal layering, atmospheric synthesizers, and intricate percussion.</p>

<p>Enya performs the main title theme <a href="/the-celts/#The_Celts">"The Celts"</a> at the start of every episode, and is glimpsed at the end of the opening credit sequence.</p>

<p>The broadcast also featured standalone music video interludes for <a href="/video-i-want-tomorrow/">"I Want Tomorrow"</a> and <a href="/video-aldebaran/">"Aldebaran"</a>.</p>

<p>The performance for <a href="/video-i-want-tomorrow/">"I Want Tomorrow"</a> stands out for its bold imagery, featuring Enya wearing leather biker attire in one scene and shooting fire from her fingertips in another.</p>

<p>The track "Aldebaran" was inspired by the brightest star in the <strong>Taurus</strong> constellation, representing a futuristic Celtic migration across space.</p>

<p>The 2004 <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0000WN10E?tag=enylyrallenys-20&camp=0&creative=0&linkCode=as1&creativeASIN=B0000WN10E&adid=0SZED06FETQ0ZM78A5FX&">DVD release</a> capitalized on her star power by including exclusive contemporary interviews and rare performance footage.</p>

<h2>Soundtrack Releases and Remasters</h2>

<p>A 39-minute selection of the score was released in March 1987 as her debut solo album <a href="/enya-album/"><em>Enya</em></a> by <strong>BBC Records</strong> in the UK and <strong>Atlantic Records</strong> in the United States.</p>

<p>The album reached number 8 in Ireland and number 69 in the UK, capturing the attention of Warner Music chairman <strong>Rob Dickins</strong>, who subsequently signed her to a major label contract.</p>

<h3>The 1992 Remastered Reissue</h3>

<p>In November 1992, WEA and <strong>Reprise Records</strong> reissued the remastered soundtrack under the new title <a href="/the-celts/"><em>The Celts</em></a>, featuring fresh artwork and photography by <strong>David Scheinmann</strong>.</p>

<p>The reissued album reached number 10 on the <strong>UK Albums Chart</strong> and earned Platinum certifications in both the United Kingdom and the United States.</p>

<p>In addition to improved audio fidelity, <a href="/the-celts/"><em>The Celts</em></a> substituted the original 1:23 instrumental track "Portrait" with a longer 3:12 version titled "Portrait (Out of the Blue)".</p>

<h2>Unreleased Music and Cultural Legacy</h2>

<p>Because the original album included less than 40 minutes of Enya's 72-minute total score, a substantial amount of music from the documentary remained officially unreleased.</p>

<p>According to lyricist <a href="/roma-ryan-nicky/">Roma Ryan</a>, director David Richardson requested specific pastiche styles to match the show's visuals, ranging from Spaghetti Westerns to film noir homages.</p>

<h3>Key Soundtrack Milestones</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>"The Celts" Single:</strong> Released in November 1992 to promote the reissued album, reaching number 29 in the UK alongside a music video filmed at <strong>Bodiam Castle</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>"Eclipse" B-side:</strong> The B-side for "The Celts" single, titled "Eclipse", was created by playing the soundtrack piece "Deireadh An Tuath" backwards.</li>
<li><strong>"Spaghetti Western Theme":</strong> The lost instrumental "Spaghetti Western Theme from The Celts" was finally released on the 2005 <a href="/enya-amarantine/#Amarantine">"Amarantine"</a> single in memory of producer Tony McAuley.</li>
<li><strong>"Boadicea" Sampling:</strong> Named after Queen <strong>Boudica</strong> of the Iceni tribe, the instrumental track "Boadicea" became famous after being sampled by <strong>The Fugees</strong> on "Ready or Not", by <strong>Mario Winans</strong> on "I Don't Wanna Know", and by <strong>Metro Boomin</strong> on "Creepin'".</li>
</ul>
</Layout>
